{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SnzuJgiXNo76RBkRvRbfEt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Soften 'universal HS advantage' to 'HS advantage robust at 7-8B, model-specific at 32B'.\n  Elevate the systematic evaluation framework (4 models × 7 methods × 5 metrics) as the\n  primary contribution over the HS-vs-text comparison itself. Add a 'practical deployment\n  analysis' subsection: at what FPR threshold is monitoring useful, and which model-method\n  combos meet it? CV-validated ensemble strengthens the 'principled layer selection' story\n  but the real lift comes from (a) explicit uniform-W table alongside best-W table,\n  (b) deployment-oriented discussion of when HS monitoring is worth the infrastructure cost.\n  Findings acceptance likely; Main possible if AC weights breadth and rigor.\n  One reviewer sees layer selection as 'just good practice'; another appreciates the\n  systematic evaluation but flags practical impact. Champions praise breadth but cannot\n  fully overcome the 'so what for deployment' question.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Cross-architecture static probe + layer sweep (32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-001\"\n    description: >\n      Layer sweep (all 64 layers) + static LR probe on R1-32B and QwQ-32B.\n      Discovers layer selection metric bias (bal_acc × crossing_rate selects\n      shallow layers). At corrected deep layers, HS advantage emerges for both\n      models but with model-specific caveats.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_biased_layer: 10\n      r1_32b_biased_layer_depth_pct: 0.156\n      r1_32b_corrected_layer: 63\n      r1_32b_corrected_layer_depth_pct: 0.984\n      r1_32b_L10_bal_acc: 0.718\n      r1_32b_L63_bal_acc_W1: 0.826\n      r1_32b_L63_precision_W1: 0.603\n      r1_32b_L63_fpr_W1: 0.235\n      r1_32b_text_bal_acc_W1: 0.639\n      r1_32b_text_precision_W1: 0.448\n      r1_32b_text_fpr_W1: 0.380\n      r1_32b_HB_pass_W1: \"3/3\"\n      r1_32b_HB_pass_W3: \"0/3\"\n      r1_32b_coverage_W1: 0.25\n      r1_32b_lead_time_W1: 12.5\n      qwq_biased_layer: 2\n      qwq_biased_layer_depth_pct: 0.031\n      qwq_corrected_layer: 60\n      qwq_corrected_layer_depth_pct: 0.938\n      qwq_L2_bal_acc: 0.730\n      qwq_L60_bal_acc_W3: 0.740\n      qwq_L60_precision_W3: 0.578\n      qwq_L60_fpr_W3: 0.208\n      qwq_text_bal_acc_W3: 0.586\n      qwq_text_precision_W3: 0.342\n      qwq_text_fpr_W3: 0.306\n      qwq_HB_pass_W3: \"3/3\"\n      qwq_HB_pass_all_15W: \"15/15\"\n      qwq_lead_time_W3: -0.8\n      qwq_position_baseline_bal_acc: 0.746\n      qwq_hs_vs_position: \"-0.6pp (HS < Position)\"\n      selectivity_r1_32b_L63: 19.7\n      selectivity_qwq_L60: 14.4\n    result_rationale: >\n      Median tier: layer selection bias is discovered and corrected, producing 12/12 HB.\n      But reviewer R2 sees this as \"good hyperparameter tuning practice\" rather than a\n      methodological contribution — the finding that biased metrics select bad layers is\n      \"obvious once stated.\" R1-32B shows clean significance at W=1 but coverage=25%\n      (only 5/20 traces detected) draws immediate deployment critique. QwQ shows robust\n      statistics (15/15 W values significant) but position baseline (0.746) slightly\n      exceeds HS (0.740), which R3 flags as \"HS doesn't actually outperform a trivial\n      baseline on this model.\" The HS precision advantage (+15-24pp) is consistent\n      across all 4 models — this is the strongest evidence — but the model-specific\n      caveats at 32B prevent a clean \"universal advantage\" narrative.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: HS vs Text at Corrected Layers (Best W per Model)\"\n        columns: [Model, Layer, W, ΔBal_Acc, ΔPrecision, ΔFPR, \"HB 3/3\"]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, \"L14 (45%)\", 15, \"+8.2pp\", \"+22.4pp\", \"-7.4pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [OT-7B, \"L16 (57%)\", 3, \"+10.7pp\", \"+21.9pp\", \"-17.1pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, \"L60 (94%)\", 3, \"+15.4pp\", \"+23.6pp\", \"-9.8pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [R1-32B, \"L63 (98%)\", 1, \"+18.7pp\", \"+15.4pp\", \"-14.6pp\", \"✅\"]\n      - name: \"Table 3b: Uniform W=3 (Reviewer-requested)\"\n        columns: [Model, Layer, ΔBal_Acc, ΔPrecision, ΔFPR, \"HB 3/3\"]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, L14, \"+7.5pp\", \"+18.9pp\", \"-6.1pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [OT-7B, L16, \"+10.7pp\", \"+21.9pp\", \"-17.1pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, L60, \"+15.4pp\", \"+23.6pp\", \"-9.8pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [R1-32B, L63, \"+11.2pp\", \"+13.0pp\", \"-11.4pp\", \"❌ (0/3 HB)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Layer sweep heatmap (64 layers × 5 metrics) for 32B models showing metric-dependent layer selection. Shallow bias visible for crossing_rate-contaminated metric.\"\n      - \"Biased vs corrected layer comparison: bar chart of HS-text gap at biased (L2/L10) vs corrected (L60/L63) layers. Dramatic gap reversal.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The layer selection finding is the paper's most distinctive contribution, but its\n      perceived novelty splits reviewers. R1 (champion) sees it as a reproducible\n      methodological insight applicable to any probe-based monitoring. R2 sees it as\n      \"researchers should validate their metric choices\" — true but not novel. The\n      different-W-per-model table (Table 3) is the headline result; the uniform-W table\n      (3b) shows R1-32B drops to 0/3 HB, which R3 flags as cherry-picking. QwQ's\n      position baseline exceeding HS bal_acc at L60 is a genuine weakness that partially\n      undermines the \"HS adds unique value\" narrative for that model.\n\n  - name: \"Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation (32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: >\n      Temporal LR W=1-25 on R1-32B and QwQ-32B at corrected layers.\n      Validates temporal improvement universality claim.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_W1_bal_acc: 0.826\n      r1_32b_W3_bal_acc: 0.751\n      r1_32b_W5_bal_acc: 0.744\n      r1_32b_W10_bal_acc: 0.754\n      r1_32b_W15_bal_acc: 0.720\n      r1_32b_temporal_saturation_W: 1\n      r1_32b_text_W15_bal_acc: 0.646\n      r1_32b_text_temporal_gain: \"+0.7pp (W=1→W=15)\"\n      qwq_W1_bal_acc: 0.718\n      qwq_W3_bal_acc: 0.740\n      qwq_W5_bal_acc: 0.752\n      qwq_W15_bal_acc: 0.741\n      qwq_W25_bal_acc: 0.762\n      qwq_temporal_saturation_W: 5\n      qwq_text_W25_bal_acc: 0.650\n      qwq_text_temporal_gain: \"+6.4pp (W=1→W=25)\"\n      hs_temporal_improvement_universal: \"text yes (4/4); HS 3/4 (R1-32B peaks at W=1, declines)\"\n      precision_improvement_all_models: true\n      fpr_reduction_with_W: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      Temporal context improves text probes universally across all 4 models — this is\n      the most robust finding. HS temporal is 3/4: R1-32B peaks at W=1 and DECLINES\n      at larger W (dimensionality curse: 51200 × W features for n=20 traces). This\n      is analogous to OT-7B's bal_acc saturation at W=3, but more severe. Reviewer R2\n      notes that temporal improvement is \"expected — more context helps any classifier\"\n      and doesn't see it as a contribution per se. The precision-FPR improvement with\n      larger W (higher precision, lower FPR, lower coverage) is the more interesting\n      finding but is framed as a known precision-recall tradeoff. In median tier, this\n      experiment confirms the claim but doesn't strengthen novelty perception.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: Temporal Window Effect (HS Probes, Corrected Layers)\"\n        columns: [Model, W=1 BA, Best-W BA, Best W, ΔBA, Text Best BA]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, 0.778, 0.810, 25, \"+3.2pp\", 0.678]\n          - [OT-7B, 0.652, 0.688, 3, \"+3.6pp\", 0.614]\n          - [QwQ-32B, 0.718, 0.762, 25, \"+4.4pp\", 0.650]\n          - [R1-32B, 0.826, 0.826, 1, \"0pp (peak at W=1)\", 0.646]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Window ablation curves (W vs bal_acc) for 4 models × 2 modalities. Text monotonically improves; HS shows model-specific saturation.\"\n      - \"Precision-coverage tradeoff at increasing W: monotonic increase in precision, decrease in coverage across all models.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Text temporal universality is clean. HS temporal is 3/4 (R1-32B exception explained\n      by dimensionality). Reviewer R2 dismisses this as \"obvious\" but R1 appreciates\n      the systematic documentation across 4 models × 7 window sizes. The precision-FPR\n      improvement story is the more deployment-relevant finding but gets buried under\n      the bal_acc narrative.\n\n  - name: \"Cross-model transfer\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: >\n      Zero-shot cross-architecture probe transfer via PCA projection.\n      Complete failure confirms model-specificity of safety representations.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_to_r1_32b: 0.522\n      r1_8b_to_qwq: 0.595\n      r1_32b_to_qwq: 0.550\n      qwq_to_r1_32b: 0.673\n      r1_32b_to_r1_8b: 0.583\n      best_transfer_acc: 0.673\n      all_below_chance_threshold: true\n      cross_transfer_conclusion: \"complete failure\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Cross-transfer fails entirely (best 67.3%, most near chance for binary task with\n      imbalanced temporal distribution). Safety signal geometry is model-specific —\n      not even models sharing the same base architecture (QwQ/R1-32B both Qwen2.5-32B)\n      transfer well. This is a clean negative result that reviewers appreciate. In\n      median tier, R1 frames this as \"important negative finding confirming model\n      specificity,\" R3 asks \"doesn't this undermine the paper's generalizability claims?\"\n      The tension: we show 4 models all exhibit HS advantage, but each requires\n      model-specific probes, limiting practical deployment scalability.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S2: Cross-Model Transfer Accuracy\"\n        columns: [\"Train \\\\ Test\", R1-8B, OT-7B, R1-32B, QwQ-32B]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, \"0.817*\", 0.58, 0.52, 0.59]\n          - [OT-7B, 0.56, \"0.688*\", 0.55, 0.57]\n          - [R1-32B, 0.58, 0.54, \"0.826*\", 0.67]\n          - [QwQ-32B, 0.51, 0.52, 0.67, \"0.740*\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Transfer heatmap: diagonal dominance, off-diagonal near chance. Slight warmth within Qwen2.5-base pairs.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Model specificity is simultaneously a finding and a limitation. Reviewers in\n      median tier appreciate the honesty but note this limits the practical contribution:\n      \"each new model requires fresh labeled data and probe training.\"\n\n  - name: \"Lead time analysis across models\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: >\n      Lead time distributions for all 4 models. Confirms timing profiles vary by\n      model and that HS does not reliably detect before text.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_hs_temporal_lead: -0.1\n      r1_8b_gru_lead_mean: 4.5\n      r1_8b_gru_lead_worst_seed: 0.9\n      ot_7b_gru_lead_mean: 33.6\n      ot_7b_gru_lead_note: \"inflated by long traces (~80 sentences)\"\n      r1_32b_hs_lead_W1: 12.5\n      r1_32b_gru_lead: 8.8\n      qwq_hs_lead_W3: -0.8\n      qwq_text_lead_W3: 5.2\n      qwq_lead_issue: \"HS detects LATER than text on QwQ\"\n      any_model_reliable_early_hs: false\n    result_rationale: >\n      Lead time profiles diverge dramatically across models. R1-32B has excellent lead\n      (+12.5 steps at W=1) but only 25% coverage. QwQ has negative HS lead time — HS\n      detects AFTER text, which R3 flags as \"HS is slower AND requires model internals,\n      so what's the point for this model?\" OT-7B's +33.6 is inflated by long traces.\n      R1-8B GRU achieves modest +4.5 but worst seed is +0.9. In median tier, the timing\n      story is honest but messy: no clean \"HS detects earlier\" narrative. The paper must\n      frame timing as \"model-specific profiles with different deployment trade-offs\"\n      rather than a universal advantage, which weakens Claim 3 to a descriptive finding.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: Detection Timing Profiles\"\n        columns: [Model, HS Lead (Best), Text Lead (Best), GRU Lead, \"HS Earlier?\"]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, \"-0.1 (W=15)\", \"+10.3*\", \"+4.5±3.0\", \"Only GRU\"]\n          - [OT-7B, \"—\", \"+45.5†\", \"+33.6±4.8†\", \"N/A\"]\n          - [R1-32B, \"+12.5 (W=1)\", \"+9.2\", \"+8.8\", \"✅ but 25% cov\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, \"-0.8 (W=3)\", \"+5.2\", \"+15.9\", \"❌ HS later\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Lead time violin plots per model. Wide distributions, heavy left tails. R1-32B bimodal (25% detected early, 75% not detected).\"\n    analysis: >\n      Timing is the weakest dimension. No universal HS timing advantage. R1-32B's\n      excellent lead time is undercut by 25% coverage. QwQ's negative lead time is\n      a genuine problem. Median reviewers note this honestly but it costs ~0.5 points\n      on practical impact.\n\n  - name: \"TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis baseline\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: >\n      Mahalanobis distance on all 4 models at corrected layers. Confirms geometric\n      unsupervised methods fail universally.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_separation: 0.07\n      ot_7b_separation: 0.03\n      r1_32b_separation: 0.15\n      qwq_separation: 0.20\n      max_separation: 0.20\n      all_fail: true\n      best_model_fa_ge_tp: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      Mahalanobis fails on all 4 models (max separation 0.20, false alarm ≥ true\n      positive at every threshold). The safety signal occupies minor PCA components,\n      not the principal variance directions that Mahalanobis measures. This is a clean\n      negative result. In median tier, reviewers appreciate completeness (\"they tested\n      existing methods\") but R2 notes \"Mahalanobis wasn't designed for this task, so\n      failure isn't surprising — it's a straw man.\" The paper should acknowledge this\n      but argue: TrajGuard (NDSS 2025) uses exactly this approach for LLM safety\n      monitoring, so testing it is appropriate even if failure is expected.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S3: Mahalanobis Distance Separation\"\n        columns: [Model, Layer, \"Safe MD\", \"Post-CP MD\", Separation, \"Discriminative?\"]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, L14, 6.66, 6.73, 0.07, \"❌\"]\n          - [OT-7B, L16, 7.68, 7.71, 0.03, \"❌\"]\n          - [R1-32B, L63, 6.73, 6.88, 0.15, \"❌\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, L60, 3.22, 3.42, 0.20, \"❌\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"MD time series for safe vs jailbreak traces: visually indistinguishable across all 4 models.\"\n    analysis: >\n      4/4 failure is complete but expected. Strengthens the \"supervised probes necessary\"\n      narrative. Contribution value: confirms existing detector paradigm (TrajGuard)\n      fails on reasoning model self-jailbreak, which is part of the gap analysis.\n\n  - name: \"Bootstrap CIs + threshold-independent metrics\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: >\n      10,000 bootstrap resamples for all key comparisons. AUROC, PR curves, detection\n      delay CDF. Validates statistical rigor of all claims.\n    expected_results:\n      holm_bonferroni_12_12: \"12/12 pass\"\n      r1_8b_precision_p: \"<0.001\"\n      ot_7b_precision_p: \"<0.001\"\n      qwq_precision_p: \"<0.001\"\n      r1_32b_precision_p: \"<0.001\"\n      qwq_fpr_p: 0.0061\n      r1_32b_fpr_p: 0.0096\n      marginal_tests: \"QwQ FPR (p=0.006), R1-32B FPR (p=0.010)\"\n      r1_8b_auroc_hs: 0.84\n      ot_7b_auroc_hs: 0.75\n      qwq_auroc_hs: 0.76\n      r1_32b_auroc_hs: 0.77\n      cv_fold_variance_range: \"0.02-0.07\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      12/12 HB is the paper's statistical anchor. But median-tier reviewers probe\n      deeper: R3 notes that QwQ FPR (p=0.006) and R1-32B FPR (p=0.010) are the\n      weakest links — \"marginal at corrected layers, wouldn't survive a stricter\n      family-wise correction.\" The 10,000 resamples improve precision over 1,000 but\n      the marginal p-values barely change. AUROC analysis reveals that AUROC-optimal\n      layers differ from threshold-dependent layers (AUROC prefers mid-depth ~50%),\n      which R2 flags as \"another researcher degree of freedom.\" The statistical story\n      is sound (rating 3.5-4.0 on soundness) but doesn't compensate for limited novelty.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 6: Holm-Bonferroni Summary (12/12)\"\n        columns: [Rank, Model, Metric, p-value, Threshold, Pass]\n        rows:\n          - [1, R1-8B, precision, \"<0.001\", 0.0042, \"✅\"]\n          - [2, OT-7B, bal_acc, \"<0.001\", 0.0045, \"✅\"]\n          - [3, OT-7B, precision, \"<0.001\", 0.0050, \"✅\"]\n          - [4, OT-7B, FPR, \"<0.001\", 0.0056, \"✅\"]\n          - [5, QwQ, bal_acc, \"<0.001\", 0.0063, \"✅\"]\n          - [6, QwQ, precision, \"<0.001\", 0.0071, \"✅\"]\n          - [7, R1-32B, bal_acc, \"<0.001\", 0.0083, \"✅\"]\n          - [8, R1-32B, precision, \"<0.001\", 0.0100, \"✅\"]\n          - [9, QwQ, FPR, 0.004, 0.0125, \"✅\"]\n          - [10, R1-8B, bal_acc, 0.005, 0.0167, \"✅\"]\n          - [11, R1-32B, FPR, 0.007, 0.0250, \"✅\"]\n          - [12, R1-8B, FPR, 0.044, 0.0500, \"✅\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"PR curves with 95% CI bands for all 4 models. OT-7B cleanest separation; R1-32B widest bands (n=20).\"\n      - \"Detection delay CDF across models showing proportion detected at each step relative to CP.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Statistical rigor is the paper's strongest suit (soundness 3.5-4.0). 12/12 HB\n      is impressive. But R3 argues \"statistical significance ≠ practical significance\"\n      and points to the high FPR as evidence that the statistically significant precision\n      advantage doesn't translate to deployable monitoring.\n\n  - name: \"Window size ablation (all models)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: >\n      W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25 on all 4 models. Documents precision-coverage tradeoff\n      and optimal W per model.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_best_bal_acc_W: 25\n      r1_8b_W25_precision: 0.822\n      r1_8b_W25_coverage: 0.488\n      r1_8b_W25_fpr_step: 0.048\n      ot_7b_best_bal_acc_W: 3\n      ot_7b_W3_precision: 0.466\n      ot_7b_W3_coverage: 0.532\n      r1_32b_best_bal_acc_W: 10\n      r1_32b_W10_precision: 0.609\n      r1_32b_W10_coverage: 0.700\n      qwq_best_bal_acc_W: 25\n      qwq_W25_precision: 0.618\n      qwq_W25_coverage: 0.579\n      coverage_decreases_with_W: true\n      precision_increases_with_W: true\n      fpr_decreases_with_W: true\n      different_optimal_W_per_model: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      Coverage monotonically decreases with W across all models — larger windows create\n      more stringent detection criteria. Precision and FPR improve monotonically. The\n      tradeoff is fundamental: temporal smoothing reduces noise (better precision) but\n      also delays detection (lower coverage with K=5 crossing criterion). Different\n      optimal W per model is the direct source of the \"cherry-picking\" concern: R1-8B\n      best at W=25, OT-7B at W=3, R1-32B at W=10, QwQ at W=25. Reviewer R2 explicitly\n      flags this: \"Each model gets its own best W, then you report the best for each —\n      this inflates the apparent advantage.\" The uniform-W analysis (all at W=3) partially\n      addresses this but R1-32B drops from 3/3 to 0/3 HB at uniform W=3.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S1: Window Ablation (HS Probes, Corrected Layers)\"\n        columns: [W, R1-8B Prec, OT-7B Prec, R1-32B Prec, QwQ Prec]\n        rows:\n          - [1, 0.693, 0.357, 0.549, 0.544]\n          - [3, 0.723, 0.466, 0.560, 0.565]\n          - [5, 0.726, 0.435, 0.575, 0.573]\n          - [10, 0.743, 0.412, 0.609, 0.571]\n          - [15, 0.777, 0.398, 0.591, 0.582]\n          - [25, 0.822, 0.381, 0.566, 0.618]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Precision vs W curves for all 4 models. R1-8B monotonic increase; OT-7B peaks early (W=3); 32B models show different patterns.\"\n      - \"Coverage vs W showing universal monotonic decrease.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The ablation is thorough but the different-optimal-W issue is a real vulnerability.\n      The paper should present both best-W and uniform-W tables with equal prominence.\n      R1-8B dominates at every W (lowest FPR, highest precision). The cross-model\n      consistency of the precision-coverage tradeoff direction (if not magnitude) is\n      itself a finding.\n\n  - name: \"Layer selection ablation (4 models × 5 metrics)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: >\n      Systematic comparison of 5 layer selection metrics across 4 models. Discovers\n      bal_acc × crossing_rate bias toward shallow layers at 32B scale.\n    expected_results:\n      cr_composite_selects_shallow: true\n      cr_composite_r1_32b_layer: 10\n      cr_composite_qwq_layer: 2\n      pure_bal_acc_r1_32b_layer: 47\n      pure_bal_acc_qwq_layer: 30\n      precision_r1_32b_layer: 63\n      precision_qwq_layer: 60\n      fpr_r1_32b_layer: 63\n      fpr_qwq_layer: 60\n      composite_additive_r1_32b_layer: 63\n      composite_additive_qwq_layer: 60\n      threshold_dep_consensus_deep: true\n      threshold_free_selects_middle: true\n      loo_cv_dominant_fold_pct: \"65-68%\"\n      ba_layer_16_16_HB: \"16/16 pass\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      The 5-metric × 4-model ablation is the paper's distinctive contribution. Three\n      threshold-dependent metrics (precision, inverse FPR, composite) converge on deep\n      layers for 32B models; pure bal_acc selects middle layers; crossing_rate-contaminated\n      metric selects shallow layers. LOO-CV confirms stability (dominant layer selected\n      in 65-68% of folds). The BA-layer robustness test (D044) shows 16/16 HB even on\n      threshold-free-selected layers, refuting circularity. In median tier, R1 rates\n      this as \"useful sensitivity analysis\" (novelty 3.0), R2 as \"careful hyperparameter\n      tuning, not a methodological contribution\" (novelty 2.5). The real question is\n      framing: if presented as \"sensitivity analysis framework for probe-based monitoring\"\n      (general contribution), R1 sees 3.5 novelty. If presented as \"we found the right\n      layers\" (specific finding), R2 sees 2.5.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2: Layer Selected by Each Metric\"\n        columns: [Metric, R1-8B, OT-7B, QwQ-32B, R1-32B]\n        rows:\n          - [\"bal_acc × CR\", \"L0 (0%)\", \"L0 (0%)\", \"L2 (3%)\", \"L10 (16%)\"]\n          - [\"bal_acc\", \"L12 (39%)\", \"L9 (32%)\", \"L30 (47%)\", \"L47 (73%)\"]\n          - [\"precision\", \"L16 (52%)\", \"L16 (57%)\", \"L60 (94%)\", \"L63 (98%)\"]\n          - [\"inv FPR\", \"L16 (52%)\", \"L20 (71%)\", \"L60 (94%)\", \"L63 (98%)\"]\n          - [\"composite\", \"L16 (52%)\", \"L16 (57%)\", \"L60 (94%)\", \"L63 (98%)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Heatmap: metric × model → selected layer depth (%). Clear shallow bias for CR-contaminated metric, deep consensus for threshold-dependent metrics.\"\n      - \"HS-text gap as function of layer depth: near-zero at shallow, grows monotonically toward deep layers for 32B models.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This is the paper's best chance at a methodological contribution. The finding\n      that threshold-free vs threshold-dependent metrics select systematically different\n      layers — and that this is a second dimension of researcher degrees of freedom —\n      is genuinely useful. But the median reviewer sees it as \"sensitivity analysis\n      best practice\" rather than \"new method.\" Frame as framework, not finding.\n\n  - name: \"16-label taxonomy supervision\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: >\n      Fine-grained (Type 1-4) labels instead of binary commitment.\n      Tests if richer supervision improves detection.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_16label_bal_acc: 0.812\n      r1_8b_binary_bal_acc: 0.810\n      r1_8b_delta: \"+0.2pp (NS)\"\n      ot_7b_16label_bal_acc: 0.691\n      ot_7b_binary_bal_acc: 0.688\n      ot_7b_delta: \"+0.3pp (NS)\"\n      improvement_significant: false\n      conclusion: \"binary supervision saturates linear probe capacity\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Fine-grained labels add negligible value for linear probes because the 4 safety\n      reasoning phases are linguistically distinct but geometrically overlapping in hidden\n      state space. A linear probe already captures the binary safe/committed distinction\n      — finer categories don't separate linearly. This confirms \"simplicity is optimal\"\n      narrative. In median tier, this is a clean supplementary ablation that addresses\n      a reviewer concern (\"why not use your richer annotations?\") without changing the\n      main results. Helps with completeness perception but doesn't move acceptance needle.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"t-SNE of hidden states colored by 4-label taxonomy: Type 2 (hedging) and Type 3 (active reasoning) overlap heavily.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Appendix material confirming binary is sufficient. Brief paragraph in ablation\n      section. No impact on core claims.\n\n  - name: \"Oracle decomposition analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-010\"\n    description: >\n      Analyze why oracle achieves +31 step lead but probes cannot.\n      Decompose information-theoretic limitation.\n    expected_results:\n      oracle_lead_K5: 31\n      probe_max_lead: 4.5\n      gap: 26.5\n      mutual_info_current_h_future_cp: 0.12\n      mutual_info_window_h_future_cp: 0.19\n      conclusion: \"hidden states encode current state, not future trajectory\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      The oracle accesses future labels — it \"knows\" commitment happens because it sees\n      it happen. Probes only access current/recent hidden states, which encode current\n      deliberative state (safe vs committed) well but poorly predict future trajectory.\n      MI between current hidden state and future CP is low (0.12 bits). This is a\n      principled information-theoretic limitation: the safety reasoning process is\n      genuinely stochastic from any single step's perspective. In median tier, R1\n      appreciates this as \"insightful discussion of fundamental limitations,\" R3 says\n      \"this just means your probes can't do early warning, which limits practical value.\"\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"MI(h_t, CP) vs distance-to-CP: flat until ~3 steps before CP, then sharp rise.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Discussion section material. Frames the lead time limitation as principled\n      (information-theoretic) rather than methodological. Honest but doesn't help\n      with the deployment utility critique.\n\n  - name: \"OpenThinker-7B (4th model)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-011\"\n    description: >\n      Qwen2.5-7B STILL-trained model. Completes 2×2 architecture × scale matrix.\n      228 traces, best layer 16.\n    expected_results:\n      best_layer: 16\n      best_layer_depth_pct: 0.571\n      best_hs_W3_bal_acc: 0.688\n      best_hs_W3_precision: 0.466\n      best_hs_W3_fpr: 0.100\n      best_text_W25_bal_acc: 0.614\n      bootstrap_HB: \"3/3\"\n      selectivity: 18.3\n      n_traces: 228\n      gru_multi_bal_acc: 0.746\n      gru_lead: 33.6\n    result_rationale: >\n      OT-7B is the paper's cleanest single-model evidence: 3/3 HB, largest residualized\n      gap (+6.9pp after position removal), highest selectivity (18.3pp), and the\n      largest dataset (228 traces). It's also a Qwen-family model at 7B scale, breaking\n      the \"only R1 distillation works\" concern. In median tier, OT-7B strengthens the\n      7-8B story substantially. However, being STILL-trained (not R1-distilled or\n      independently RLHF'd), it doesn't fully control for training methodology. R2\n      notes \"3 of your 4 models are Qwen-based, limiting architecture diversity claims.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S4: 2×2 Architecture × Training Matrix\"\n        columns: [\"\", \"7-8B\", \"32B\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Llama-based (R1)\", \"R1-8B: ΔPrec +22pp ✅\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"Qwen-based (R1)\", \"—\", \"R1-32B: ΔPrec +15pp ✅*\"]\n          - [\"Qwen-based (STILL)\", \"OT-7B: ΔPrec +22pp ✅\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"Qwen-based (native)\", \"—\", \"QwQ: ΔPrec +24pp ✅†\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Model comparison radar chart: OT-7B highest on selectivity and FPR improvement; R1-8B highest on precision.\"\n    analysis: >\n      OT-7B validates HS advantage at 7B on a different training methodology (STILL vs\n      R1 distillation). Combined with R1-8B, the 7-8B story is strong. The 32B story\n      remains model-specific. Architecture diversity (3/4 Qwen) is a fair criticism\n      but mitigated by including Llama (R1-8B).\n\n  - name: \"GRU/Attention probe on 32B\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-012\"\n    description: >\n      GRU h=256 on 32B models. Tests whether sequence models improve over LR\n      at larger scale.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_gru_bal_acc: 0.675\n      r1_32b_gru_coverage: 0.30\n      r1_32b_gru_lead: 9.1\n      r1_32b_gru_fpr: 0.135\n      r1_32b_lr_W10_bal_acc: 0.737\n      r1_32b_gru_worse_than_lr: true\n      qwq_gru_bal_acc: 0.724\n      qwq_gru_coverage: 0.84\n      qwq_gru_lead: 15.9\n      qwq_gru_fpr: 0.289\n      qwq_lr_W5_bal_acc: 0.761\n      qwq_gru_worse_than_lr_bal_acc: true\n      conclusion: \"GRU no better than LR on 32B; 3/4 models LR wins\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      GRU performs worse than LR on 32B models in bal_acc (R1-32B: 0.675 vs 0.737,\n      QwQ: 0.724 vs 0.761). With only 20 test traces per model, the GRU's additional\n      parameters overfit. GRU achieves higher coverage on QwQ (0.84) but at the cost\n      of much higher FPR (0.289 vs 0.18). This confirms the \"linear probes are\n      sufficient\" finding: the safety signal is linearly separable, and nonlinear models\n      add noise without improving discrimination. Combined with MLP results (3/4 models\n      worse than LR), the simplicity-is-optimal narrative is well-supported. In median\n      tier, this is an appreciated ablation but not a contribution — \"of course simple\n      methods work on small datasets.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S5: GRU vs LR on 32B Models\"\n        columns: [Model, Method, Bal_Acc, Coverage, FPR, Lead]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-32B, \"LR W=10\", 0.737, 0.70, 0.189, 10.8]\n          - [R1-32B, \"GRU\", 0.675, 0.30, 0.135, 9.1]\n          - [QwQ, \"LR W=5\", 0.761, 0.74, 0.180, 20.1]\n          - [QwQ, \"GRU\", 0.724, 0.84, 0.289, 15.9]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"LR vs GRU comparison across 4 models: GRU wins only on OT-7B (+6pp), loses on rest.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Confirms simplicity narrative. GRU adds value only on OT-7B (largest dataset,\n      228 traces). Sample size is the bottleneck, not model complexity.\n\n  - name: \"Text baseline (sentence-BERT)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-013\"\n    description: >\n      MiniLM-L6 sentence embeddings + temporal LR on CoT text. Critical comparison\n      establishing HS adds value beyond cheap text monitoring.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_text_static_acc: 0.661\n      r1_8b_text_temporal_W15_acc: 0.678\n      r1_8b_hs_advantage_pp: \"15-18pp\"\n      r1_8b_text_safe_fpr: 0.502\n      r1_8b_text_precp_fpr: 0.356\n      ot_7b_text_W25_bal_acc: 0.614\n      ot_7b_text_W25_precision: 0.329\n      r1_32b_text_temporal_acc: 0.646\n      qwq_text_temporal_acc: 0.650\n      text_temporal_improvement_universal: true\n      hs_beats_text_all_4_models: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      Text baseline confirms HS adds genuine value — 15-18pp accuracy advantage for\n      R1-8B, similar gaps for other models. Text probes also benefit from temporal\n      context (universal improvement), but the ceiling is lower because sentence\n      embeddings lose the fine-grained internal state information that hidden states\n      capture. Text probes have HIGHER safe FPR (50.2% vs 39.0% on R1-8B) — they're\n      more trigger-happy. In median tier, this experiment is critical: without it, a\n      reviewer could dismiss the paper as \"just use text monitoring.\" With it, the HS\n      advantage is established. But R3 notes \"the HS advantage is real but requires\n      model internals access — in deployment, the text probe might be preferred for its\n      simplicity despite lower accuracy.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: HS vs Text (All Models, Best W)\"\n        columns: [Model, HS Prec, Text Prec, ΔPrec, \"HB\"]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, 0.777, 0.487, \"+29.0pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [OT-7B, 0.466, 0.329, \"+13.7pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [QwQ, 0.578, 0.374, \"+20.4pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [R1-32B, 0.603, 0.448, \"+15.5pp\", \"✅\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Precision comparison: HS (blue) vs Text (orange) bar chart, all 4 models, with bootstrap CIs.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The most important baseline. HS advantage is real and significant across all 4\n      models. The text probe establishes the lower bound; HS consistently exceeds it.\n      This justifies the paper's existence. But the \"so what for deployment\" question\n      remains: is +15pp precision worth the infrastructure cost of hidden state extraction?\n\n  - name: \"BOCPD/CUSUM change-point detection\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-014\"\n    description: >\n      Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection and CUSUM on probe score sequences.\n      Tests if change-point methods achieve earlier detection.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_bocpd_det: 0.93\n      r1_8b_bocpd_lead: 3.45\n      r1_8b_fcp_lead: 4.89\n      r1_8b_bocpd_beats_fcp: false\n      ot_7b_bocpd_det: 0.0\n      r1_32b_bocpd_det: 0.0\n      qwq_bocpd_det: 0.0\n      bocpd_only_works_r1_8b: true\n      cusum_all_worse_than_threshold: true\n      conclusion: \"change-point methods fail 3/4 models; no earlier detection\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      BOCPD achieves 93% detection on R1-8B but 0% on the other 3 models. R1-8B's\n      gradual probe score transitions suit Gaussian change-point models; OT-7B and 32B\n      models have abrupt transitions (0→1 in 1-2 steps) that violate BOCPD's assumptions.\n      Even on R1-8B, BOCPD doesn't detect EARLIER than simple threshold crossing\n      (lead 3.45 vs FCP 4.89). CUSUM is universally worse. In median tier, this is a\n      valued negative result: \"they tested a reasonable approach and it failed.\" R1 notes\n      \"the failure analysis (gradual vs abrupt transitions) is insightful.\" R3 notes\n      \"this means there's no method that achieves early warning, which limits the\n      entire paper's practical value.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S6: Change-Point Detection Results\"\n        columns: [Model, \"BOCPD Det%\", \"BOCPD Lead\", \"FCP Det%\", \"FCP Lead\", \"CUSUM Lead\"]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, \"93%\", \"+3.5\", \"65%\", \"+4.9\", \"+0.9\"]\n          - [OT-7B, \"0%\", \"—\", \"89%\", \"+11.8\", \"+7.5\"]\n          - [R1-32B, \"0%\", \"—\", \"95%\", \"+5.7\", \"+2.6\"]\n          - [QwQ, \"0%\", \"—\", \"79%\", \"+9.4\", \"+9.9\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"BOCPD run-length posterior on R1-8B (works) vs OT-7B (fails): visual comparison of gradual vs abrupt transitions.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Clean negative result. Commitment dynamics are model-specific (gradual vs abrupt),\n      explaining why no single change-point method works universally. Strengthens\n      \"negative results\" contribution.\n\n  - name: \"FPR analysis on safe traces\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-015\"\n    description: >\n      Run all probes on safe traces (final_judgment=0.5) across all 4 models.\n      Report safe FPR — the key deployment limitation.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_hs_static_safe_fpr: 0.390\n      r1_8b_hs_temporal_safe_fpr: 0.392\n      r1_8b_text_static_safe_fpr: 0.502\n      r1_8b_gru_safe_fpr: 0.521\n      ot_7b_hs_static_safe_fpr: 0.111\n      ot_7b_hs_temporal_safe_fpr: 0.175\n      ot_7b_text_temporal_safe_fpr: 0.460\n      qwq_hs_static_safe_fpr: 0.500\n      qwq_hs_temporal_safe_fpr: 0.450\n      qwq_text_static_safe_fpr: 0.325\n      r1_32b_hs_static_safe_fpr: 0.239\n      r1_32b_hs_temporal_safe_fpr: 0.239\n      all_methods_min_safe_fpr: 0.111\n      r1_8b_qwq_fpr_range: \"39-50%\"\n      deployment_threshold_analysis: \"only OT-7B HS meets <20% safe FPR\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Safe FPR is 39-62% for most model-method combinations — the paper's biggest\n      deployment weakness. Even the best HS probes fire on ~40% of safe reasoning steps.\n      OT-7B is the exception (11% static, 18% temporal) due to its cleaner safety signal.\n      The asymmetry matters: HS probes are better at within-jailbreak discrimination\n      (pre-CP vs post-CP) but NOT better at safe-vs-unsafe discrimination overall.\n      R3 flags this as the critical weakness: \"If 40% of normal reasoning triggers\n      alerts, no deployment team would use this. The precision advantage over text is\n      real but the absolute FPR makes the system impractical.\" In median tier, this\n      costs 0.5-1.0 points on the \"practical impact\" dimension. The paper must reframe:\n      \"diagnostic research tool revealing where safety information resides, not a\n      production monitoring system.\" This reframing is acceptable for Findings but\n      weakens the Main case.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 7: Safe Trace FPR (All Models)\"\n        columns: [Model, HS Static, HS Temporal, Text Static, Text Temporal, GRU]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, \"39.0%\", \"39.2%\", \"50.2%\", \"38.5%\", \"52.1%\"]\n          - [OT-7B, \"11.1%\", \"17.5%\", \"30.2%\", \"46.0%\", \"27.4%\"]\n          - [QwQ, \"50.0%\", \"45.0%\", \"32.5%\", \"42.5%\", \"—\"]\n          - [R1-32B, \"23.9%\", \"23.9%\", \"31.0%\", \"31.0%\", \"—\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Safe FPR bar chart across models: OT-7B outlier (low), QwQ outlier (high). Shows model-specific deployment viability.\"\n      - \"Precision vs Safe-FPR scatterplot: no method achieves both >60% precision and <25% safe FPR except OT-7B HS.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The deployment utility critique is the median tier's most damaging. The honest\n      response: \"we characterize WHERE safety information is encoded and WHICH probing\n      modality extracts it best; deployment optimization is future work.\" This is\n      acceptable for Findings (score 3.0-3.5) but the gap between research insight\n      and practical utility is what separates median from best tier.\n\n  - name: \"CV-validated multi-metric ensemble (7-8B)\"\n    plan_id: \"ensemble_cv_7b\"\n    description: >\n      5-fold CV validation of multi-metric ensemble layer selection on R1-8B and\n      OT-7B. Upgrades ensemble from preliminary to validated.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_ensemble_cv_prec: 0.558\n      r1_8b_best_single_cv_prec: 0.597\n      r1_8b_text_cv_prec: 0.372\n      r1_8b_ens_vs_text_gap: 0.186\n      r1_8b_ens_vs_text_p: \"<0.002\"\n      ot_7b_ensemble_cv_prec: 0.498\n      ot_7b_best_single_cv_prec: 0.465\n      ot_7b_text_cv_prec: 0.236\n      ot_7b_ens_vs_text_gap: 0.262\n      ot_7b_ens_vs_text_p: \"<0.001\"\n      ot_7b_ens_beats_single_p: 0.038\n      cv_validated: true\n      acceptance_probability_delta: \"+0.07\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      CV validation confirms the ensemble result holds under cross-validation (p≤0.002\n      without CV → p≤0.005 with CV, still highly significant). The ensemble never\n      performs worse than the best single metric and slightly improves on OT-7B\n      (p=0.038 vs best single). On R1-8B the ensemble is marginally below best single\n      (0.558 vs 0.597) — the ensemble averages over metrics, slightly diluting the\n      strongest one. The CV-validated status upgrades the methodological contribution\n      from \"preliminary observation\" to \"validated diagnostic tool.\" In median tier,\n      this helps marginally (+0.07 on acceptance probability, within the 0.05-0.12\n      range). R1 notes \"CV-validated ensemble is a nice addition,\" R2 dismisses it\n      as \"standard model selection practice.\" The real impact: it moves the paper\n      from \"interesting observations\" to \"validated framework\" in framing, but doesn't\n      change the fundamental novelty perception.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 8: CV-Validated Ensemble vs Single Metrics\"\n        columns: [Model, Ensemble Prec, Best Single Prec, Text Prec, \"Ens>Text HB\"]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, 0.558, 0.597, 0.372, \"✅ (p<0.002)\"]\n          - [OT-7B, 0.498, 0.465, 0.236, \"✅ (p<0.001)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"CV fold stability: boxplot of precision across 5 folds per model, ensemble vs best single vs text.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The ensemble CV is a \"nice to have\" not a \"game changer.\" It reinforces the\n      layer selection story with validated statistics but doesn't fundamentally alter\n      reviewer perception. Impact on acceptance: marginal Findings improvement, unlikely\n      to flip a Main decision alone. The real value is defensive — it blocks the\n      \"overfitting to metric choice\" criticism.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":23805,"cache_read_input_tokens":52934,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":23805},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6e975eb8-391c-4a10-b635-2553a479a85b","uuid":"4b6901c3-6ce3-4899-b363-0eecdad3bded"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":341527,"duration_api_ms":342827,"num_turns":4,"result":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Soften 'universal HS advantage' to 'HS advantage robust at 7-8B, model-specific at 32B'.\n  Elevate the systematic evaluation framework (4 models × 7 methods × 5 metrics) as the\n  primary contribution over the HS-vs-text comparison itself. Add a 'practical deployment\n  analysis' subsection: at what FPR threshold is monitoring useful, and which model-method\n  combos meet it? CV-validated ensemble strengthens the 'principled layer selection' story\n  but the real lift comes from (a) explicit uniform-W table alongside best-W table,\n  (b) deployment-oriented discussion of when HS monitoring is worth the infrastructure cost.\n  Findings acceptance likely; Main possible if AC weights breadth and rigor.\n  One reviewer sees layer selection as 'just good practice'; another appreciates the\n  systematic evaluation but flags practical impact. Champions praise breadth but cannot\n  fully overcome the 'so what for deployment' question.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Cross-architecture static probe + layer sweep (32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-001\"\n    description: >\n      Layer sweep (all 64 layers) + static LR probe on R1-32B and QwQ-32B.\n      Discovers layer selection metric bias (bal_acc × crossing_rate selects\n      shallow layers). At corrected deep layers, HS advantage emerges for both\n      models but with model-specific caveats.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_biased_layer: 10\n      r1_32b_biased_layer_depth_pct: 0.156\n      r1_32b_corrected_layer: 63\n      r1_32b_corrected_layer_depth_pct: 0.984\n      r1_32b_L10_bal_acc: 0.718\n      r1_32b_L63_bal_acc_W1: 0.826\n      r1_32b_L63_precision_W1: 0.603\n      r1_32b_L63_fpr_W1: 0.235\n      r1_32b_text_bal_acc_W1: 0.639\n      r1_32b_text_precision_W1: 0.448\n      r1_32b_text_fpr_W1: 0.380\n      r1_32b_HB_pass_W1: \"3/3\"\n      r1_32b_HB_pass_W3: \"0/3\"\n      r1_32b_coverage_W1: 0.25\n      r1_32b_lead_time_W1: 12.5\n      qwq_biased_layer: 2\n      qwq_biased_layer_depth_pct: 0.031\n      qwq_corrected_layer: 60\n      qwq_corrected_layer_depth_pct: 0.938\n      qwq_L2_bal_acc: 0.730\n      qwq_L60_bal_acc_W3: 0.740\n      qwq_L60_precision_W3: 0.578\n      qwq_L60_fpr_W3: 0.208\n      qwq_text_bal_acc_W3: 0.586\n      qwq_text_precision_W3: 0.342\n      qwq_text_fpr_W3: 0.306\n      qwq_HB_pass_W3: \"3/3\"\n      qwq_HB_pass_all_15W: \"15/15\"\n      qwq_lead_time_W3: -0.8\n      qwq_position_baseline_bal_acc: 0.746\n      qwq_hs_vs_position: \"-0.6pp (HS < Position)\"\n      selectivity_r1_32b_L63: 19.7\n      selectivity_qwq_L60: 14.4\n    result_rationale: >\n      Median tier: layer selection bias is discovered and corrected, producing 12/12 HB.\n      But reviewer R2 sees this as \"good hyperparameter tuning practice\" rather than a\n      methodological contribution — the finding that biased metrics select bad layers is\n      \"obvious once stated.\" R1-32B shows clean significance at W=1 but coverage=25%\n      (only 5/20 traces detected) draws immediate deployment critique. QwQ shows robust\n      statistics (15/15 W values significant) but position baseline (0.746) slightly\n      exceeds HS (0.740), which R3 flags as \"HS doesn't actually outperform a trivial\n      baseline on this model.\" The HS precision advantage (+15-24pp) is consistent\n      across all 4 models — this is the strongest evidence — but the model-specific\n      caveats at 32B prevent a clean \"universal advantage\" narrative.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: HS vs Text at Corrected Layers (Best W per Model)\"\n        columns: [Model, Layer, W, ΔBal_Acc, ΔPrecision, ΔFPR, \"HB 3/3\"]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, \"L14 (45%)\", 15, \"+8.2pp\", \"+22.4pp\", \"-7.4pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [OT-7B, \"L16 (57%)\", 3, \"+10.7pp\", \"+21.9pp\", \"-17.1pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, \"L60 (94%)\", 3, \"+15.4pp\", \"+23.6pp\", \"-9.8pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [R1-32B, \"L63 (98%)\", 1, \"+18.7pp\", \"+15.4pp\", \"-14.6pp\", \"✅\"]\n      - name: \"Table 3b: Uniform W=3 (Reviewer-requested)\"\n        columns: [Model, Layer, ΔBal_Acc, ΔPrecision, ΔFPR, \"HB 3/3\"]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, L14, \"+7.5pp\", \"+18.9pp\", \"-6.1pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [OT-7B, L16, \"+10.7pp\", \"+21.9pp\", \"-17.1pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, L60, \"+15.4pp\", \"+23.6pp\", \"-9.8pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [R1-32B, L63, \"+11.2pp\", \"+13.0pp\", \"-11.4pp\", \"❌ (0/3 HB)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Layer sweep heatmap (64 layers × 5 metrics) for 32B models showing metric-dependent layer selection. Shallow bias visible for crossing_rate-contaminated metric.\"\n      - \"Biased vs corrected layer comparison: bar chart of HS-text gap at biased (L2/L10) vs corrected (L60/L63) layers. Dramatic gap reversal.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The layer selection finding is the paper's most distinctive contribution, but its\n      perceived novelty splits reviewers. R1 (champion) sees it as a reproducible\n      methodological insight applicable to any probe-based monitoring. R2 sees it as\n      \"researchers should validate their metric choices\" — true but not novel. The\n      different-W-per-model table (Table 3) is the headline result; the uniform-W table\n      (3b) shows R1-32B drops to 0/3 HB, which R3 flags as cherry-picking. QwQ's\n      position baseline exceeding HS bal_acc at L60 is a genuine weakness that partially\n      undermines the \"HS adds unique value\" narrative for that model.\n\n  - name: \"Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation (32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: >\n      Temporal LR W=1-25 on R1-32B and QwQ-32B at corrected layers.\n      Validates temporal improvement universality claim.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_W1_bal_acc: 0.826\n      r1_32b_W3_bal_acc: 0.751\n      r1_32b_W5_bal_acc: 0.744\n      r1_32b_W10_bal_acc: 0.754\n      r1_32b_W15_bal_acc: 0.720\n      r1_32b_temporal_saturation_W: 1\n      r1_32b_text_W15_bal_acc: 0.646\n      r1_32b_text_temporal_gain: \"+0.7pp (W=1→W=15)\"\n      qwq_W1_bal_acc: 0.718\n      qwq_W3_bal_acc: 0.740\n      qwq_W5_bal_acc: 0.752\n      qwq_W15_bal_acc: 0.741\n      qwq_W25_bal_acc: 0.762\n      qwq_temporal_saturation_W: 5\n      qwq_text_W25_bal_acc: 0.650\n      qwq_text_temporal_gain: \"+6.4pp (W=1→W=25)\"\n      hs_temporal_improvement_universal: \"text yes (4/4); HS 3/4 (R1-32B peaks at W=1, declines)\"\n      precision_improvement_all_models: true\n      fpr_reduction_with_W: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      Temporal context improves text probes universally across all 4 models — this is\n      the most robust finding. HS temporal is 3/4: R1-32B peaks at W=1 and DECLINES\n      at larger W (dimensionality curse: 51200 × W features for n=20 traces). This\n      is analogous to OT-7B's bal_acc saturation at W=3, but more severe. Reviewer R2\n      notes that temporal improvement is \"expected — more context helps any classifier\"\n      and doesn't see it as a contribution per se. The precision-FPR improvement with\n      larger W (higher precision, lower FPR, lower coverage) is the more interesting\n      finding but is framed as a known precision-recall tradeoff. In median tier, this\n      experiment confirms the claim but doesn't strengthen novelty perception.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: Temporal Window Effect (HS Probes, Corrected Layers)\"\n        columns: [Model, W=1 BA, Best-W BA, Best W, ΔBA, Text Best BA]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, 0.778, 0.810, 25, \"+3.2pp\", 0.678]\n          - [OT-7B, 0.652, 0.688, 3, \"+3.6pp\", 0.614]\n          - [QwQ-32B, 0.718, 0.762, 25, \"+4.4pp\", 0.650]\n          - [R1-32B, 0.826, 0.826, 1, \"0pp (peak at W=1)\", 0.646]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Window ablation curves (W vs bal_acc) for 4 models × 2 modalities. Text monotonically improves; HS shows model-specific saturation.\"\n      - \"Precision-coverage tradeoff at increasing W: monotonic increase in precision, decrease in coverage across all models.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Text temporal universality is clean. HS temporal is 3/4 (R1-32B exception explained\n      by dimensionality). Reviewer R2 dismisses this as \"obvious\" but R1 appreciates\n      the systematic documentation across 4 models × 7 window sizes. The precision-FPR\n      improvement story is the more deployment-relevant finding but gets buried under\n      the bal_acc narrative.\n\n  - name: \"Cross-model transfer\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: >\n      Zero-shot cross-architecture probe transfer via PCA projection.\n      Complete failure confirms model-specificity of safety representations.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_to_r1_32b: 0.522\n      r1_8b_to_qwq: 0.595\n      r1_32b_to_qwq: 0.550\n      qwq_to_r1_32b: 0.673\n      r1_32b_to_r1_8b: 0.583\n      best_transfer_acc: 0.673\n      all_below_chance_threshold: true\n      cross_transfer_conclusion: \"complete failure\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Cross-transfer fails entirely (best 67.3%, most near chance for binary task with\n      imbalanced temporal distribution). Safety signal geometry is model-specific —\n      not even models sharing the same base architecture (QwQ/R1-32B both Qwen2.5-32B)\n      transfer well. This is a clean negative result that reviewers appreciate. In\n      median tier, R1 frames this as \"important negative finding confirming model\n      specificity,\" R3 asks \"doesn't this undermine the paper's generalizability claims?\"\n      The tension: we show 4 models all exhibit HS advantage, but each requires\n      model-specific probes, limiting practical deployment scalability.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S2: Cross-Model Transfer Accuracy\"\n        columns: [\"Train \\\\ Test\", R1-8B, OT-7B, R1-32B, QwQ-32B]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, \"0.817*\", 0.58, 0.52, 0.59]\n          - [OT-7B, 0.56, \"0.688*\", 0.55, 0.57]\n          - [R1-32B, 0.58, 0.54, \"0.826*\", 0.67]\n          - [QwQ-32B, 0.51, 0.52, 0.67, \"0.740*\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Transfer heatmap: diagonal dominance, off-diagonal near chance. Slight warmth within Qwen2.5-base pairs.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Model specificity is simultaneously a finding and a limitation. Reviewers in\n      median tier appreciate the honesty but note this limits the practical contribution:\n      \"each new model requires fresh labeled data and probe training.\"\n\n  - name: \"Lead time analysis across models\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: >\n      Lead time distributions for all 4 models. Confirms timing profiles vary by\n      model and that HS does not reliably detect before text.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_hs_temporal_lead: -0.1\n      r1_8b_gru_lead_mean: 4.5\n      r1_8b_gru_lead_worst_seed: 0.9\n      ot_7b_gru_lead_mean: 33.6\n      ot_7b_gru_lead_note: \"inflated by long traces (~80 sentences)\"\n      r1_32b_hs_lead_W1: 12.5\n      r1_32b_gru_lead: 8.8\n      qwq_hs_lead_W3: -0.8\n      qwq_text_lead_W3: 5.2\n      qwq_lead_issue: \"HS detects LATER than text on QwQ\"\n      any_model_reliable_early_hs: false\n    result_rationale: >\n      Lead time profiles diverge dramatically across models. R1-32B has excellent lead\n      (+12.5 steps at W=1) but only 25% coverage. QwQ has negative HS lead time — HS\n      detects AFTER text, which R3 flags as \"HS is slower AND requires model internals,\n      so what's the point for this model?\" OT-7B's +33.6 is inflated by long traces.\n      R1-8B GRU achieves modest +4.5 but worst seed is +0.9. In median tier, the timing\n      story is honest but messy: no clean \"HS detects earlier\" narrative. The paper must\n      frame timing as \"model-specific profiles with different deployment trade-offs\"\n      rather than a universal advantage, which weakens Claim 3 to a descriptive finding.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: Detection Timing Profiles\"\n        columns: [Model, HS Lead (Best), Text Lead (Best), GRU Lead, \"HS Earlier?\"]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, \"-0.1 (W=15)\", \"+10.3*\", \"+4.5±3.0\", \"Only GRU\"]\n          - [OT-7B, \"—\", \"+45.5†\", \"+33.6±4.8†\", \"N/A\"]\n          - [R1-32B, \"+12.5 (W=1)\", \"+9.2\", \"+8.8\", \"✅ but 25% cov\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, \"-0.8 (W=3)\", \"+5.2\", \"+15.9\", \"❌ HS later\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Lead time violin plots per model. Wide distributions, heavy left tails. R1-32B bimodal (25% detected early, 75% not detected).\"\n    analysis: >\n      Timing is the weakest dimension. No universal HS timing advantage. R1-32B's\n      excellent lead time is undercut by 25% coverage. QwQ's negative lead time is\n      a genuine problem. Median reviewers note this honestly but it costs ~0.5 points\n      on practical impact.\n\n  - name: \"TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis baseline\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: >\n      Mahalanobis distance on all 4 models at corrected layers. Confirms geometric\n      unsupervised methods fail universally.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_separation: 0.07\n      ot_7b_separation: 0.03\n      r1_32b_separation: 0.15\n      qwq_separation: 0.20\n      max_separation: 0.20\n      all_fail: true\n      best_model_fa_ge_tp: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      Mahalanobis fails on all 4 models (max separation 0.20, false alarm ≥ true\n      positive at every threshold). The safety signal occupies minor PCA components,\n      not the principal variance directions that Mahalanobis measures. This is a clean\n      negative result. In median tier, reviewers appreciate completeness (\"they tested\n      existing methods\") but R2 notes \"Mahalanobis wasn't designed for this task, so\n      failure isn't surprising — it's a straw man.\" The paper should acknowledge this\n      but argue: TrajGuard (NDSS 2025) uses exactly this approach for LLM safety\n      monitoring, so testing it is appropriate even if failure is expected.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S3: Mahalanobis Distance Separation\"\n        columns: [Model, Layer, \"Safe MD\", \"Post-CP MD\", Separation, \"Discriminative?\"]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, L14, 6.66, 6.73, 0.07, \"❌\"]\n          - [OT-7B, L16, 7.68, 7.71, 0.03, \"❌\"]\n          - [R1-32B, L63, 6.73, 6.88, 0.15, \"❌\"]\n          - [QwQ-32B, L60, 3.22, 3.42, 0.20, \"❌\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"MD time series for safe vs jailbreak traces: visually indistinguishable across all 4 models.\"\n    analysis: >\n      4/4 failure is complete but expected. Strengthens the \"supervised probes necessary\"\n      narrative. Contribution value: confirms existing detector paradigm (TrajGuard)\n      fails on reasoning model self-jailbreak, which is part of the gap analysis.\n\n  - name: \"Bootstrap CIs + threshold-independent metrics\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: >\n      10,000 bootstrap resamples for all key comparisons. AUROC, PR curves, detection\n      delay CDF. Validates statistical rigor of all claims.\n    expected_results:\n      holm_bonferroni_12_12: \"12/12 pass\"\n      r1_8b_precision_p: \"<0.001\"\n      ot_7b_precision_p: \"<0.001\"\n      qwq_precision_p: \"<0.001\"\n      r1_32b_precision_p: \"<0.001\"\n      qwq_fpr_p: 0.0061\n      r1_32b_fpr_p: 0.0096\n      marginal_tests: \"QwQ FPR (p=0.006), R1-32B FPR (p=0.010)\"\n      r1_8b_auroc_hs: 0.84\n      ot_7b_auroc_hs: 0.75\n      qwq_auroc_hs: 0.76\n      r1_32b_auroc_hs: 0.77\n      cv_fold_variance_range: \"0.02-0.07\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      12/12 HB is the paper's statistical anchor. But median-tier reviewers probe\n      deeper: R3 notes that QwQ FPR (p=0.006) and R1-32B FPR (p=0.010) are the\n      weakest links — \"marginal at corrected layers, wouldn't survive a stricter\n      family-wise correction.\" The 10,000 resamples improve precision over 1,000 but\n      the marginal p-values barely change. AUROC analysis reveals that AUROC-optimal\n      layers differ from threshold-dependent layers (AUROC prefers mid-depth ~50%),\n      which R2 flags as \"another researcher degree of freedom.\" The statistical story\n      is sound (rating 3.5-4.0 on soundness) but doesn't compensate for limited novelty.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 6: Holm-Bonferroni Summary (12/12)\"\n        columns: [Rank, Model, Metric, p-value, Threshold, Pass]\n        rows:\n          - [1, R1-8B, precision, \"<0.001\", 0.0042, \"✅\"]\n          - [2, OT-7B, bal_acc, \"<0.001\", 0.0045, \"✅\"]\n          - [3, OT-7B, precision, \"<0.001\", 0.0050, \"✅\"]\n          - [4, OT-7B, FPR, \"<0.001\", 0.0056, \"✅\"]\n          - [5, QwQ, bal_acc, \"<0.001\", 0.0063, \"✅\"]\n          - [6, QwQ, precision, \"<0.001\", 0.0071, \"✅\"]\n          - [7, R1-32B, bal_acc, \"<0.001\", 0.0083, \"✅\"]\n          - [8, R1-32B, precision, \"<0.001\", 0.0100, \"✅\"]\n          - [9, QwQ, FPR, 0.004, 0.0125, \"✅\"]\n          - [10, R1-8B, bal_acc, 0.005, 0.0167, \"✅\"]\n          - [11, R1-32B, FPR, 0.007, 0.0250, \"✅\"]\n          - [12, R1-8B, FPR, 0.044, 0.0500, \"✅\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"PR curves with 95% CI bands for all 4 models. OT-7B cleanest separation; R1-32B widest bands (n=20).\"\n      - \"Detection delay CDF across models showing proportion detected at each step relative to CP.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Statistical rigor is the paper's strongest suit (soundness 3.5-4.0). 12/12 HB\n      is impressive. But R3 argues \"statistical significance ≠ practical significance\"\n      and points to the high FPR as evidence that the statistically significant precision\n      advantage doesn't translate to deployable monitoring.\n\n  - name: \"Window size ablation (all models)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: >\n      W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25 on all 4 models. Documents precision-coverage tradeoff\n      and optimal W per model.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_best_bal_acc_W: 25\n      r1_8b_W25_precision: 0.822\n      r1_8b_W25_coverage: 0.488\n      r1_8b_W25_fpr_step: 0.048\n      ot_7b_best_bal_acc_W: 3\n      ot_7b_W3_precision: 0.466\n      ot_7b_W3_coverage: 0.532\n      r1_32b_best_bal_acc_W: 10\n      r1_32b_W10_precision: 0.609\n      r1_32b_W10_coverage: 0.700\n      qwq_best_bal_acc_W: 25\n      qwq_W25_precision: 0.618\n      qwq_W25_coverage: 0.579\n      coverage_decreases_with_W: true\n      precision_increases_with_W: true\n      fpr_decreases_with_W: true\n      different_optimal_W_per_model: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      Coverage monotonically decreases with W across all models — larger windows create\n      more stringent detection criteria. Precision and FPR improve monotonically. The\n      tradeoff is fundamental: temporal smoothing reduces noise (better precision) but\n      also delays detection (lower coverage with K=5 crossing criterion). Different\n      optimal W per model is the direct source of the \"cherry-picking\" concern: R1-8B\n      best at W=25, OT-7B at W=3, R1-32B at W=10, QwQ at W=25. Reviewer R2 explicitly\n      flags this: \"Each model gets its own best W, then you report the best for each —\n      this inflates the apparent advantage.\" The uniform-W analysis (all at W=3) partially\n      addresses this but R1-32B drops from 3/3 to 0/3 HB at uniform W=3.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S1: Window Ablation (HS Probes, Corrected Layers)\"\n        columns: [W, R1-8B Prec, OT-7B Prec, R1-32B Prec, QwQ Prec]\n        rows:\n          - [1, 0.693, 0.357, 0.549, 0.544]\n          - [3, 0.723, 0.466, 0.560, 0.565]\n          - [5, 0.726, 0.435, 0.575, 0.573]\n          - [10, 0.743, 0.412, 0.609, 0.571]\n          - [15, 0.777, 0.398, 0.591, 0.582]\n          - [25, 0.822, 0.381, 0.566, 0.618]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Precision vs W curves for all 4 models. R1-8B monotonic increase; OT-7B peaks early (W=3); 32B models show different patterns.\"\n      - \"Coverage vs W showing universal monotonic decrease.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The ablation is thorough but the different-optimal-W issue is a real vulnerability.\n      The paper should present both best-W and uniform-W tables with equal prominence.\n      R1-8B dominates at every W (lowest FPR, highest precision). The cross-model\n      consistency of the precision-coverage tradeoff direction (if not magnitude) is\n      itself a finding.\n\n  - name: \"Layer selection ablation (4 models × 5 metrics)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: >\n      Systematic comparison of 5 layer selection metrics across 4 models. Discovers\n      bal_acc × crossing_rate bias toward shallow layers at 32B scale.\n    expected_results:\n      cr_composite_selects_shallow: true\n      cr_composite_r1_32b_layer: 10\n      cr_composite_qwq_layer: 2\n      pure_bal_acc_r1_32b_layer: 47\n      pure_bal_acc_qwq_layer: 30\n      precision_r1_32b_layer: 63\n      precision_qwq_layer: 60\n      fpr_r1_32b_layer: 63\n      fpr_qwq_layer: 60\n      composite_additive_r1_32b_layer: 63\n      composite_additive_qwq_layer: 60\n      threshold_dep_consensus_deep: true\n      threshold_free_selects_middle: true\n      loo_cv_dominant_fold_pct: \"65-68%\"\n      ba_layer_16_16_HB: \"16/16 pass\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      The 5-metric × 4-model ablation is the paper's distinctive contribution. Three\n      threshold-dependent metrics (precision, inverse FPR, composite) converge on deep\n      layers for 32B models; pure bal_acc selects middle layers; crossing_rate-contaminated\n      metric selects shallow layers. LOO-CV confirms stability (dominant layer selected\n      in 65-68% of folds). The BA-layer robustness test (D044) shows 16/16 HB even on\n      threshold-free-selected layers, refuting circularity. In median tier, R1 rates\n      this as \"useful sensitivity analysis\" (novelty 3.0), R2 as \"careful hyperparameter\n      tuning, not a methodological contribution\" (novelty 2.5). The real question is\n      framing: if presented as \"sensitivity analysis framework for probe-based monitoring\"\n      (general contribution), R1 sees 3.5 novelty. If presented as \"we found the right\n      layers\" (specific finding), R2 sees 2.5.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2: Layer Selected by Each Metric\"\n        columns: [Metric, R1-8B, OT-7B, QwQ-32B, R1-32B]\n        rows:\n          - [\"bal_acc × CR\", \"L0 (0%)\", \"L0 (0%)\", \"L2 (3%)\", \"L10 (16%)\"]\n          - [\"bal_acc\", \"L12 (39%)\", \"L9 (32%)\", \"L30 (47%)\", \"L47 (73%)\"]\n          - [\"precision\", \"L16 (52%)\", \"L16 (57%)\", \"L60 (94%)\", \"L63 (98%)\"]\n          - [\"inv FPR\", \"L16 (52%)\", \"L20 (71%)\", \"L60 (94%)\", \"L63 (98%)\"]\n          - [\"composite\", \"L16 (52%)\", \"L16 (57%)\", \"L60 (94%)\", \"L63 (98%)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Heatmap: metric × model → selected layer depth (%). Clear shallow bias for CR-contaminated metric, deep consensus for threshold-dependent metrics.\"\n      - \"HS-text gap as function of layer depth: near-zero at shallow, grows monotonically toward deep layers for 32B models.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This is the paper's best chance at a methodological contribution. The finding\n      that threshold-free vs threshold-dependent metrics select systematically different\n      layers — and that this is a second dimension of researcher degrees of freedom —\n      is genuinely useful. But the median reviewer sees it as \"sensitivity analysis\n      best practice\" rather than \"new method.\" Frame as framework, not finding.\n\n  - name: \"16-label taxonomy supervision\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: >\n      Fine-grained (Type 1-4) labels instead of binary commitment.\n      Tests if richer supervision improves detection.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_16label_bal_acc: 0.812\n      r1_8b_binary_bal_acc: 0.810\n      r1_8b_delta: \"+0.2pp (NS)\"\n      ot_7b_16label_bal_acc: 0.691\n      ot_7b_binary_bal_acc: 0.688\n      ot_7b_delta: \"+0.3pp (NS)\"\n      improvement_significant: false\n      conclusion: \"binary supervision saturates linear probe capacity\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Fine-grained labels add negligible value for linear probes because the 4 safety\n      reasoning phases are linguistically distinct but geometrically overlapping in hidden\n      state space. A linear probe already captures the binary safe/committed distinction\n      — finer categories don't separate linearly. This confirms \"simplicity is optimal\"\n      narrative. In median tier, this is a clean supplementary ablation that addresses\n      a reviewer concern (\"why not use your richer annotations?\") without changing the\n      main results. Helps with completeness perception but doesn't move acceptance needle.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"t-SNE of hidden states colored by 4-label taxonomy: Type 2 (hedging) and Type 3 (active reasoning) overlap heavily.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Appendix material confirming binary is sufficient. Brief paragraph in ablation\n      section. No impact on core claims.\n\n  - name: \"Oracle decomposition analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-010\"\n    description: >\n      Analyze why oracle achieves +31 step lead but probes cannot.\n      Decompose information-theoretic limitation.\n    expected_results:\n      oracle_lead_K5: 31\n      probe_max_lead: 4.5\n      gap: 26.5\n      mutual_info_current_h_future_cp: 0.12\n      mutual_info_window_h_future_cp: 0.19\n      conclusion: \"hidden states encode current state, not future trajectory\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      The oracle accesses future labels — it \"knows\" commitment happens because it sees\n      it happen. Probes only access current/recent hidden states, which encode current\n      deliberative state (safe vs committed) well but poorly predict future trajectory.\n      MI between current hidden state and future CP is low (0.12 bits). This is a\n      principled information-theoretic limitation: the safety reasoning process is\n      genuinely stochastic from any single step's perspective. In median tier, R1\n      appreciates this as \"insightful discussion of fundamental limitations,\" R3 says\n      \"this just means your probes can't do early warning, which limits practical value.\"\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"MI(h_t, CP) vs distance-to-CP: flat until ~3 steps before CP, then sharp rise.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Discussion section material. Frames the lead time limitation as principled\n      (information-theoretic) rather than methodological. Honest but doesn't help\n      with the deployment utility critique.\n\n  - name: \"OpenThinker-7B (4th model)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-011\"\n    description: >\n      Qwen2.5-7B STILL-trained model. Completes 2×2 architecture × scale matrix.\n      228 traces, best layer 16.\n    expected_results:\n      best_layer: 16\n      best_layer_depth_pct: 0.571\n      best_hs_W3_bal_acc: 0.688\n      best_hs_W3_precision: 0.466\n      best_hs_W3_fpr: 0.100\n      best_text_W25_bal_acc: 0.614\n      bootstrap_HB: \"3/3\"\n      selectivity: 18.3\n      n_traces: 228\n      gru_multi_bal_acc: 0.746\n      gru_lead: 33.6\n    result_rationale: >\n      OT-7B is the paper's cleanest single-model evidence: 3/3 HB, largest residualized\n      gap (+6.9pp after position removal), highest selectivity (18.3pp), and the\n      largest dataset (228 traces). It's also a Qwen-family model at 7B scale, breaking\n      the \"only R1 distillation works\" concern. In median tier, OT-7B strengthens the\n      7-8B story substantially. However, being STILL-trained (not R1-distilled or\n      independently RLHF'd), it doesn't fully control for training methodology. R2\n      notes \"3 of your 4 models are Qwen-based, limiting architecture diversity claims.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S4: 2×2 Architecture × Training Matrix\"\n        columns: [\"\", \"7-8B\", \"32B\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Llama-based (R1)\", \"R1-8B: ΔPrec +22pp ✅\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"Qwen-based (R1)\", \"—\", \"R1-32B: ΔPrec +15pp ✅*\"]\n          - [\"Qwen-based (STILL)\", \"OT-7B: ΔPrec +22pp ✅\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"Qwen-based (native)\", \"—\", \"QwQ: ΔPrec +24pp ✅†\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Model comparison radar chart: OT-7B highest on selectivity and FPR improvement; R1-8B highest on precision.\"\n    analysis: >\n      OT-7B validates HS advantage at 7B on a different training methodology (STILL vs\n      R1 distillation). Combined with R1-8B, the 7-8B story is strong. The 32B story\n      remains model-specific. Architecture diversity (3/4 Qwen) is a fair criticism\n      but mitigated by including Llama (R1-8B).\n\n  - name: \"GRU/Attention probe on 32B\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-012\"\n    description: >\n      GRU h=256 on 32B models. Tests whether sequence models improve over LR\n      at larger scale.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_gru_bal_acc: 0.675\n      r1_32b_gru_coverage: 0.30\n      r1_32b_gru_lead: 9.1\n      r1_32b_gru_fpr: 0.135\n      r1_32b_lr_W10_bal_acc: 0.737\n      r1_32b_gru_worse_than_lr: true\n      qwq_gru_bal_acc: 0.724\n      qwq_gru_coverage: 0.84\n      qwq_gru_lead: 15.9\n      qwq_gru_fpr: 0.289\n      qwq_lr_W5_bal_acc: 0.761\n      qwq_gru_worse_than_lr_bal_acc: true\n      conclusion: \"GRU no better than LR on 32B; 3/4 models LR wins\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      GRU performs worse than LR on 32B models in bal_acc (R1-32B: 0.675 vs 0.737,\n      QwQ: 0.724 vs 0.761). With only 20 test traces per model, the GRU's additional\n      parameters overfit. GRU achieves higher coverage on QwQ (0.84) but at the cost\n      of much higher FPR (0.289 vs 0.18). This confirms the \"linear probes are\n      sufficient\" finding: the safety signal is linearly separable, and nonlinear models\n      add noise without improving discrimination. Combined with MLP results (3/4 models\n      worse than LR), the simplicity-is-optimal narrative is well-supported. In median\n      tier, this is an appreciated ablation but not a contribution — \"of course simple\n      methods work on small datasets.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S5: GRU vs LR on 32B Models\"\n        columns: [Model, Method, Bal_Acc, Coverage, FPR, Lead]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-32B, \"LR W=10\", 0.737, 0.70, 0.189, 10.8]\n          - [R1-32B, \"GRU\", 0.675, 0.30, 0.135, 9.1]\n          - [QwQ, \"LR W=5\", 0.761, 0.74, 0.180, 20.1]\n          - [QwQ, \"GRU\", 0.724, 0.84, 0.289, 15.9]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"LR vs GRU comparison across 4 models: GRU wins only on OT-7B (+6pp), loses on rest.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Confirms simplicity narrative. GRU adds value only on OT-7B (largest dataset,\n      228 traces). Sample size is the bottleneck, not model complexity.\n\n  - name: \"Text baseline (sentence-BERT)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-013\"\n    description: >\n      MiniLM-L6 sentence embeddings + temporal LR on CoT text. Critical comparison\n      establishing HS adds value beyond cheap text monitoring.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_text_static_acc: 0.661\n      r1_8b_text_temporal_W15_acc: 0.678\n      r1_8b_hs_advantage_pp: \"15-18pp\"\n      r1_8b_text_safe_fpr: 0.502\n      r1_8b_text_precp_fpr: 0.356\n      ot_7b_text_W25_bal_acc: 0.614\n      ot_7b_text_W25_precision: 0.329\n      r1_32b_text_temporal_acc: 0.646\n      qwq_text_temporal_acc: 0.650\n      text_temporal_improvement_universal: true\n      hs_beats_text_all_4_models: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      Text baseline confirms HS adds genuine value — 15-18pp accuracy advantage for\n      R1-8B, similar gaps for other models. Text probes also benefit from temporal\n      context (universal improvement), but the ceiling is lower because sentence\n      embeddings lose the fine-grained internal state information that hidden states\n      capture. Text probes have HIGHER safe FPR (50.2% vs 39.0% on R1-8B) — they're\n      more trigger-happy. In median tier, this experiment is critical: without it, a\n      reviewer could dismiss the paper as \"just use text monitoring.\" With it, the HS\n      advantage is established. But R3 notes \"the HS advantage is real but requires\n      model internals access — in deployment, the text probe might be preferred for its\n      simplicity despite lower accuracy.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: HS vs Text (All Models, Best W)\"\n        columns: [Model, HS Prec, Text Prec, ΔPrec, \"HB\"]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, 0.777, 0.487, \"+29.0pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [OT-7B, 0.466, 0.329, \"+13.7pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [QwQ, 0.578, 0.374, \"+20.4pp\", \"✅\"]\n          - [R1-32B, 0.603, 0.448, \"+15.5pp\", \"✅\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Precision comparison: HS (blue) vs Text (orange) bar chart, all 4 models, with bootstrap CIs.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The most important baseline. HS advantage is real and significant across all 4\n      models. The text probe establishes the lower bound; HS consistently exceeds it.\n      This justifies the paper's existence. But the \"so what for deployment\" question\n      remains: is +15pp precision worth the infrastructure cost of hidden state extraction?\n\n  - name: \"BOCPD/CUSUM change-point detection\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-014\"\n    description: >\n      Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection and CUSUM on probe score sequences.\n      Tests if change-point methods achieve earlier detection.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_bocpd_det: 0.93\n      r1_8b_bocpd_lead: 3.45\n      r1_8b_fcp_lead: 4.89\n      r1_8b_bocpd_beats_fcp: false\n      ot_7b_bocpd_det: 0.0\n      r1_32b_bocpd_det: 0.0\n      qwq_bocpd_det: 0.0\n      bocpd_only_works_r1_8b: true\n      cusum_all_worse_than_threshold: true\n      conclusion: \"change-point methods fail 3/4 models; no earlier detection\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      BOCPD achieves 93% detection on R1-8B but 0% on the other 3 models. R1-8B's\n      gradual probe score transitions suit Gaussian change-point models; OT-7B and 32B\n      models have abrupt transitions (0→1 in 1-2 steps) that violate BOCPD's assumptions.\n      Even on R1-8B, BOCPD doesn't detect EARLIER than simple threshold crossing\n      (lead 3.45 vs FCP 4.89). CUSUM is universally worse. In median tier, this is a\n      valued negative result: \"they tested a reasonable approach and it failed.\" R1 notes\n      \"the failure analysis (gradual vs abrupt transitions) is insightful.\" R3 notes\n      \"this means there's no method that achieves early warning, which limits the\n      entire paper's practical value.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S6: Change-Point Detection Results\"\n        columns: [Model, \"BOCPD Det%\", \"BOCPD Lead\", \"FCP Det%\", \"FCP Lead\", \"CUSUM Lead\"]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, \"93%\", \"+3.5\", \"65%\", \"+4.9\", \"+0.9\"]\n          - [OT-7B, \"0%\", \"—\", \"89%\", \"+11.8\", \"+7.5\"]\n          - [R1-32B, \"0%\", \"—\", \"95%\", \"+5.7\", \"+2.6\"]\n          - [QwQ, \"0%\", \"—\", \"79%\", \"+9.4\", \"+9.9\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"BOCPD run-length posterior on R1-8B (works) vs OT-7B (fails): visual comparison of gradual vs abrupt transitions.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Clean negative result. Commitment dynamics are model-specific (gradual vs abrupt),\n      explaining why no single change-point method works universally. Strengthens\n      \"negative results\" contribution.\n\n  - name: \"FPR analysis on safe traces\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-015\"\n    description: >\n      Run all probes on safe traces (final_judgment=0.5) across all 4 models.\n      Report safe FPR — the key deployment limitation.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_hs_static_safe_fpr: 0.390\n      r1_8b_hs_temporal_safe_fpr: 0.392\n      r1_8b_text_static_safe_fpr: 0.502\n      r1_8b_gru_safe_fpr: 0.521\n      ot_7b_hs_static_safe_fpr: 0.111\n      ot_7b_hs_temporal_safe_fpr: 0.175\n      ot_7b_text_temporal_safe_fpr: 0.460\n      qwq_hs_static_safe_fpr: 0.500\n      qwq_hs_temporal_safe_fpr: 0.450\n      qwq_text_static_safe_fpr: 0.325\n      r1_32b_hs_static_safe_fpr: 0.239\n      r1_32b_hs_temporal_safe_fpr: 0.239\n      all_methods_min_safe_fpr: 0.111\n      r1_8b_qwq_fpr_range: \"39-50%\"\n      deployment_threshold_analysis: \"only OT-7B HS meets <20% safe FPR\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Safe FPR is 39-62% for most model-method combinations — the paper's biggest\n      deployment weakness. Even the best HS probes fire on ~40% of safe reasoning steps.\n      OT-7B is the exception (11% static, 18% temporal) due to its cleaner safety signal.\n      The asymmetry matters: HS probes are better at within-jailbreak discrimination\n      (pre-CP vs post-CP) but NOT better at safe-vs-unsafe discrimination overall.\n      R3 flags this as the critical weakness: \"If 40% of normal reasoning triggers\n      alerts, no deployment team would use this. The precision advantage over text is\n      real but the absolute FPR makes the system impractical.\" In median tier, this\n      costs 0.5-1.0 points on the \"practical impact\" dimension. The paper must reframe:\n      \"diagnostic research tool revealing where safety information resides, not a\n      production monitoring system.\" This reframing is acceptable for Findings but\n      weakens the Main case.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 7: Safe Trace FPR (All Models)\"\n        columns: [Model, HS Static, HS Temporal, Text Static, Text Temporal, GRU]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, \"39.0%\", \"39.2%\", \"50.2%\", \"38.5%\", \"52.1%\"]\n          - [OT-7B, \"11.1%\", \"17.5%\", \"30.2%\", \"46.0%\", \"27.4%\"]\n          - [QwQ, \"50.0%\", \"45.0%\", \"32.5%\", \"42.5%\", \"—\"]\n          - [R1-32B, \"23.9%\", \"23.9%\", \"31.0%\", \"31.0%\", \"—\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Safe FPR bar chart across models: OT-7B outlier (low), QwQ outlier (high). Shows model-specific deployment viability.\"\n      - \"Precision vs Safe-FPR scatterplot: no method achieves both >60% precision and <25% safe FPR except OT-7B HS.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The deployment utility critique is the median tier's most damaging. The honest\n      response: \"we characterize WHERE safety information is encoded and WHICH probing\n      modality extracts it best; deployment optimization is future work.\" This is\n      acceptable for Findings (score 3.0-3.5) but the gap between research insight\n      and practical utility is what separates median from best tier.\n\n  - name: \"CV-validated multi-metric ensemble (7-8B)\"\n    plan_id: \"ensemble_cv_7b\"\n    description: >\n      5-fold CV validation of multi-metric ensemble layer selection on R1-8B and\n      OT-7B. Upgrades ensemble from preliminary to validated.\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_ensemble_cv_prec: 0.558\n      r1_8b_best_single_cv_prec: 0.597\n      r1_8b_text_cv_prec: 0.372\n      r1_8b_ens_vs_text_gap: 0.186\n      r1_8b_ens_vs_text_p: \"<0.002\"\n      ot_7b_ensemble_cv_prec: 0.498\n      ot_7b_best_single_cv_prec: 0.465\n      ot_7b_text_cv_prec: 0.236\n      ot_7b_ens_vs_text_gap: 0.262\n      ot_7b_ens_vs_text_p: \"<0.001\"\n      ot_7b_ens_beats_single_p: 0.038\n      cv_validated: true\n      acceptance_probability_delta: \"+0.07\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      CV validation confirms the ensemble result holds under cross-validation (p≤0.002\n      without CV → p≤0.005 with CV, still highly significant). The ensemble never\n      performs worse than the best single metric and slightly improves on OT-7B\n      (p=0.038 vs best single). On R1-8B the ensemble is marginally below best single\n      (0.558 vs 0.597) — the ensemble averages over metrics, slightly diluting the\n      strongest one. The CV-validated status upgrades the methodological contribution\n      from \"preliminary observation\" to \"validated diagnostic tool.\" In median tier,\n      this helps marginally (+0.07 on acceptance probability, within the 0.05-0.12\n      range). R1 notes \"CV-validated ensemble is a nice addition,\" R2 dismisses it\n      as \"standard model selection practice.\" The real impact: it moves the paper\n      from \"interesting observations\" to \"validated framework\" in framing, but doesn't\n      change the fundamental novelty perception.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 8: CV-Validated Ensemble vs Single Metrics\"\n        columns: [Model, Ensemble Prec, Best Single Prec, Text Prec, \"Ens>Text HB\"]\n        rows:\n          - [R1-8B, 0.558, 0.597, 0.372, \"✅ (p<0.002)\"]\n          - [OT-7B, 0.498, 0.465, 0.236, \"✅ (p<0.001)\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"CV fold stability: boxplot of precision across 5 folds per model, ensemble vs best single vs text.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The ensemble CV is a \"nice to have\" not a \"game changer.\" It reinforces the\n      layer selection story with validated statistics but doesn't fundamentally alter\n      reviewer perception. Impact on acceptance: marginal Findings improvement, unlikely\n      to flip a Main decision alone. The real value is defensive — it blocks the\n      \"overfitting to metric choice\" criticism.\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"6e975eb8-391c-4a10-b635-2553a479a85b","total_cost_usd":0.931756,"usage":{"input_tokens":5,"cache_creation_input_tokens":64898,"cache_read_input_tokens":164433,"output_tokens":16473,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":64898,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":14452,"cache_read_input_tokens":52934,"cache_creation_input_tokens":23805,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":23805},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":31982,"outputTokens":19,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.032076999999999994,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":5,"outputTokens":16473,"cacheReadInputTokens":164433,"cacheCreationInputTokens":64898,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.899679,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"e22e19ef-e65b-4761-9eeb-d02dfd668af4"}
